<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Solution-wide build settings. MSBuild imports this automatically for every
  project beneath it, so a setting that belongs to the whole tree lives here
  rather than being copy-pasted into six .vcxproj files and drifting.

  SupportJustMyCode
    OFF. /JMC injects a call to __CheckForDebuggerJustMyCode at the top of
    EVERY function so the debugger can step over frames that are not yours.
    That is a fine trade in most codebases and a poor one here: the 6502
    inner loop is a chain of tiny functions (StepOne, FetchOperand, ReadByte,
    FindDevice, ResolveByte, vector::operator[]), several of them per
    emulated cycle, and each one now pays a probe.

    Profiling one Debug run of DOS33_SAVE_RoundTripsToDsk put
    __CheckForDebuggerJustMyCode at 24.5% of all samples, the single largest
    leaf in the process, ahead of Cpu::StepOne itself at 9.1%.

    Turning it off costs Just My Code stepping in the debugger: call stacks
    show library frames, and F10 steps into them. It changes nothing else.
    No optimization, no iterator debugging, no _DEBUG behavior, no asserts.
    Release never sets /JMC anyway, so scoping this to Debug would only add
    noise.

    It has to be ClCompile METADATA, not just the property of the same name:
    the property alone left /JMC on all 26 command lines. And it has to be
    here rather than in Directory.Build.targets, because an ItemDefinitionGroup
    only reaches items declared after it, and ClCompile items are declared by
    the .vcxproj itself. This file is imported first; that one is imported
    last.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <SupportJustMyCode>false</SupportJustMyCode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <SupportJustMyCode>false</SupportJustMyCode>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>

</Project>
